<item>
  <title>The Spectral Theorem for Symmetric Matrices</title>
  <link>https://terryfxh.github.io/math-notes/posts/spectral-theorem/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<p>This post is a <strong>template</strong>: it demonstrates every formatting feature these notes use — inline and display math, numbered equations, theorem/proof environments, cross-references, citations, and a runnable code cell. Copy this folder to start a new post.</p>
<section id="setup" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="setup">Setup</h2>
<p>Let <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%20%5Cin%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%5E%7Bn%5Ctimes%20n%7D"> be <strong>symmetric</strong>, i.e.&nbsp;<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5E%5Ctop%20=%20A">. We write the standard inner product as <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clangle%20x,%20y%5Crangle%20=%20x%5E%5Ctop%20y">. Symmetry is equivalent to self-adjointness:</p>
<p><span id="eq-selfadjoint"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Clangle%20Ax,%20y%5Crangle%20=%20%5Clangle%20x,%20Ay%5Crangle%20%5Cqquad%20%5Ctext%7Bfor%20all%20%7D%20x,y%20%5Cin%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%5En.%0A%5Ctag%7B1%7D"></span></p>
<p>The whole theorem flows from Equation&nbsp;1.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-result" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-result">The result</h2>
<div id="thm-spectral" class="theorem">
<p><span class="theorem-title"><strong>Theorem 1 (Spectral Theorem)</strong></span> If <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%20%5Cin%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%5E%7Bn%5Ctimes%20n%7D"> is symmetric, then there exist an orthogonal matrix <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Q"> (so <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Q%5E%5Ctop%20Q%20=%20I">) and a real diagonal matrix <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5CLambda%20=%20%5Coperatorname%7Bdiag%7D(%5Clambda_1,%5Cdots,%5Clambda_n)"> such that <span id="eq-decomp"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA%20=%20Q%20%5CLambda%20Q%5E%5Ctop%20.%0A%5Ctag%7B2%7D"></span> Equivalently, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%5En"> has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A">, and every eigenvalue is real.</p>
</div>
<p>We build to it with two lemmas.</p>
<div id="lem-real" class="theorem lemma">
<p><span class="theorem-title"><strong>Lemma 1 (Eigenvalues are real)</strong></span> Every eigenvalue of a real symmetric <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A"> is real.</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><span class="proof-title"><em>Proof</em>. </span>Let <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Ax%20=%20%5Clambda%20x"> with <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x%20%5Cneq%200">, allowing complex <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda,%20x">. Using the conjugate transpose <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x%5E*"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5E%5Ctop%20=%20A"> with real entries, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Clambda%20%5C,%5Clangle%20x,%20x%5Crangle%20=%20x%5E*%20A%20x%20=%20(A%20x)%5E*%20x%20%5Cbig/%20%5C%20%5Ccdots%5C%20=%20%5Cbar%5Clambda%5C,%20%5Clangle%20x,%20x%5Crangle%20.%0A"> Since <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clangle%20x,x%5Crangle%20=%20%5C%7Cx%5C%7C%5E2%20%3E%200">, we get <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda%20=%20%5Cbar%5Clambda">, so <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda%20%5Cin%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D">. <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cqquad%5Cblacksquare"></p>
</div>
<div id="lem-orthogonal" class="theorem lemma">
<p><span class="theorem-title"><strong>Lemma 2 (Eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal)</strong></span> If <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Ax%20=%20%5Clambda%20x"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Ay%20=%20%5Cmu%20y"> with <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda%20%5Cneq%20%5Cmu">, then <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clangle%20x,%20y%5Crangle%20=%200">.</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><span class="proof-title"><em>Proof</em>. </span>By Equation&nbsp;1, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Clambda%20%5Clangle%20x,%20y%5Crangle%20=%20%5Clangle%20Ax,%20y%5Crangle%20=%20%5Clangle%20x,%20Ay%5Crangle%20=%20%5Cmu%20%5Clangle%20x,%20y%5Crangle,%0A"> so <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(%5Clambda%20-%20%5Cmu)%5Clangle%20x,%20y%5Crangle%20=%200">. As <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda%20%5Cneq%20%5Cmu">, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clangle%20x,y%5Crangle%20=%200">. <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cqquad%5Cblacksquare"></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><span class="proof-title"><em>Proof</em> (Proof of Theorem&nbsp;1 (sketch)). </span>Induct on <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n">. The base case <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n=1"> is immediate. For the step, Lemma&nbsp;1 gives a real eigenvalue <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda_1"> with unit eigenvector <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?q_1">. Its orthogonal complement <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?q_1%5E%5Cperp"> is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A">-invariant (by Equation&nbsp;1), so <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A"> restricts to a symmetric operator on an <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(n-1)">-dimensional space. The inductive hypothesis supplies an orthonormal eigenbasis there; adjoining <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?q_1"> yields an orthonormal eigenbasis of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%5En">. Collecting these vectors as columns of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Q"> gives Equation&nbsp;2. <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cqquad%5Cblacksquare"></p>
</div>
<p>A complete treatment, including the self-adjoint operator version on Hilbert spaces, is in <span class="citation" data-cites="rudin1991functional">[1]</span>; for the numerical side see <span class="citation" data-cites="trefethen1997numerical">[2]</span>.</p>
</section>
<section id="numerical-check" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="numerical-check">Numerical check</h2>
<p>Decompositions should be tested, not trusted. We build a random symmetric matrix, diagonalize it, and confirm Equation&nbsp;2 holds to machine precision.</p>
<div id="fig-spectral" class="cell quarto-float quarto-figure quarto-figure-center anchored" data-execution_count="1">
<figure class="quarto-float quarto-float-fig figure">
<div aria-describedby="fig-spectral-caption-0ceaefa1-69ba-4598-a22c-09a6ac19f8ca">
<div class="code-copy-outer-scaffold"><div class="sourceCode cell-code" id="cb1" style="background: #f1f3f5;"><pre class="sourceCode python code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode python"><span id="cb1-1"><span class="im" style="color: #00769E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">import</span> numpy <span class="im" style="color: #00769E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">as</span> np</span>
<span id="cb1-2"></span>
<span id="cb1-3">rng <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> np.random.default_rng(<span class="dv" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">0</span>)</span>
<span id="cb1-4">n <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> <span class="dv" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">6</span></span>
<span id="cb1-5">M <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> rng.standard_normal((n, n))</span>
<span id="cb1-6">A <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> (M <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">+</span> M.T) <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">/</span> <span class="dv" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">2</span>                      <span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;"># symmetrize</span></span>
<span id="cb1-7"></span>
<span id="cb1-8">eigvals, Q <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> np.linalg.eigh(A)         <span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;"># eigh exploits symmetry</span></span>
<span id="cb1-9">Lambda <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> np.diag(eigvals)</span>
<span id="cb1-10"></span>
<span id="cb1-11">reconstruction_error <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> np.linalg.norm(A <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">-</span> Q <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">@</span> Lambda <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">@</span> Q.T)</span>
<span id="cb1-12">orthogonality_error  <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">=</span> np.linalg.norm(Q.T <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">@</span> Q <span class="op" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">-</span> np.eye(n))</span>
<span id="cb1-13"></span>
<span id="cb1-14"><span class="bu" style="color: null;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">print</span>(<span class="ss" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">f"eigenvalues (all real): </span><span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">{</span>np<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">.</span><span class="bu" style="color: null;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">round</span>(eigvals, <span class="dv" style="color: #AD0000;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">4</span>)<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">}</span><span class="ss" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"</span>)</span>
<span id="cb1-15"><span class="bu" style="color: null;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">print</span>(<span class="ss" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">f"|A - Q Λ Qᵀ|  = </span><span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">{</span>reconstruction_error<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">:.2e}</span><span class="ss" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"</span>)</span>
<span id="cb1-16"><span class="bu" style="color: null;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">print</span>(<span class="ss" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">f"|QᵀQ - I|     = </span><span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">{</span>orthogonality_error<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">:.2e}</span><span class="ss" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"</span>)</span></code></pre></div></div>
<div class="cell-output cell-output-stdout">
<pre><code>eigenvalues (all real): [-2.0037 -0.988   0.2761  0.4913  1.5001  1.8151]
|A - Q Λ Qᵀ|  = 1.62e-15
|QᵀQ - I|     = 1.39e-15</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="quarto-float-caption-bottom quarto-float-caption quarto-float-fig quarto-uncaptioned" id="fig-spectral-caption-0ceaefa1-69ba-4598-a22c-09a6ac19f8ca">
Figure&nbsp;1
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<p>Both residuals sit near <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?10%5E%7B-15%7D">, confirming the decomposition numerically.</p>
</section>
<section id="takeaways" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="takeaways">Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Symmetry <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5CRightarrow"> self-adjointness (Equation&nbsp;1) is the engine of the whole proof.</li>
<li>Real eigenvalues (Lemma&nbsp;1) and orthogonality (Lemma&nbsp;2) are the two ingredients.</li>
<li>In practice, call a symmetry-aware routine like <code>eigh</code>, not the general <code>eig</code>.</li>
</ul>
</section>
